# Prompting guide for research runs

This project has reached the point where prompt design materially affects mathematical productivity. The goal of a continuation prompt is not to restate the whole theorem ledger. It is to put a strong model on the correct frontier with the correct trust boundaries, regressions, and deliverable.

The active prompt is named in `README.md`. Use that prompt as the starting point unless the theorem state has materially changed.

## 1. What a good continuation prompt must contain

A strong prompt has eight parts.

### 1. Mission and quantifiers

State the actual Level I objective, not a relaxed proxy. Make clear that the problem concerns global minimum-diameter configurations and, on the proof side, every sufficiently large minimizer.

If the run is intentionally narrower, name the exact target theorem and explain how it closes or advances the Level I problem.

### 2. Authoritative files

Give a small, explicit file set and reading order. Prefer route maps and active modules over giant historical reports.

For a Target A continuation, the default should be:

1. `docs/README.md`;
2. `docs/problem/statement.md` if quantifiers need refreshing;
3. shared theory modules actually used;
4. `docs/targets/target-a/README.md`;
5. all active Target A modules it links;
6. the active prompt itself only as operational context.

Do not attach the archive, every old prompt, and every report to “be safe.” That creates duplicated frontier statements and increases the chance that a model follows superseded mathematics.

### 3. Trust instruction

Tell the model to treat audited canonical results as a trusted theorem library and to audit the new closing mathematics rather than re-proving the entire project.

This should not forbid checking an old theorem when the new argument genuinely depends on an untested corner of its hypotheses.

### 4. Exact endpoint

Write the exact inequality, rank statement, exchange theorem, or strict-diameter construction that would finish the target.

For Target A, use the current route map. Do not substitute a heuristic such as “show many defects” or an impossible fixed-hull Hall surplus.

For Target B, use the exact shell-stability endpoint in its route map rather than an older intermediate train-count statement.

### 5. Binding corrections and dead routes

List only the failures that a capable model is likely to rediscover. Explain why each is dead in one sentence.

Examples of high-value warnings include:

- source and scaffold are not automatically co-embedded;
- fixed-hull Hall capacity is exact and cannot be exceeded inside the same hull;
- planarity/local cages do not by themselves force Schur nullity;
- many local candidate exchanges are not automatically mutually compatible;
- near-equilateral configurations remain a valid regression even when exact equilateral triangles are forbidden;
- Kuperberg witness existence does not imply an unproved quantitative support-radius rate;
- an `O(D)` error may be fatal to a one-integer endpoint.

Do not spend half the prompt listing every historical dead end.

### 6. Regression tests

Give the small set of regressions relevant to the proposed route. Require the model to state which hypothesis excludes each apparent counterexample.

If a new run is focused on a local transition theorem, include local cage and closed-semicircle regressions. If it is focused on Hall/Schur transport, include full-rank planar response and overlapping-circuit regressions. If it is a strain/Kuperberg run, include distributed strain and vanishing-gap support complexity.

### 7. Tool and literature permissions

State whether web search is allowed. When allowed, require theorem-level hypothesis checking rather than broad literature summaries.

State whether computation is allowed and what role it may play. A useful instruction is:

> Use computation to falsify intermediate universal claims, enumerate finite models, or verify exact identities. Do not treat numerical output as a global optimality certificate.

### 8. Output contract

Force the output to distinguish progress from restatement.

For an open-result run, require:

1. Level I status;
2. strongest genuinely new theorem;
3. complete proof or exact certificate;
4. target advanced;
5. exact remaining lemma;
6. regressions checked;
7. canonical changes recommended;
8. new literature and exact applicability, if any.

This structure makes parallel runs easy to deduplicate.

## 2. Prompt length

Long prompts are useful only when the text carries information that is not already in the canonical files.

Prefer pointers such as:

> Read all active Target A modules linked from `docs/targets/target-a/README.md` and treat their established results as trusted.

instead of pasting those modules into the prompt.

A prompt should contain the frontier and operating constraints. The repository should contain the mathematics.

## 3. Recommended continuation template

Copy and fill this skeleton.

```text
# Erdős Problem #99 — [target/run name]

Work in one context. Do not delegate to separate research agents unless explicitly allowed.

## Objective

[State Level I objective or exact target theorem.]

## Authoritative files

Read, in order:
1. docs/README.md
2. [shared modules actually needed]
3. docs/targets/[target]/README.md
4. every active module linked by that route map

Historical reports, archived monolithic files, and older prompts are provenance only.

## Trust instruction

Treat results explicitly marked established/audited in the active theorem modules as a trusted theorem library. Audit mathematics newly introduced in this run. Re-open an old result only if the new proof exposes a specific hypothesis issue.

## Exact endpoint

[Write the exact formula/theorem.]

Explain in one line why this endpoint closes or advances the target.

## Binding corrections

- [dead route + reason]
- [scope correction]
- [quantitative warning]

## Regression tests

Any proposed theorem must survive:
- [regression 1]
- [regression 2]
- [regression 3]

For each apparent conflict, identify the exact hypothesis the regression lacks.

## Literature/computation permissions

[Internet search allowed/not allowed.] If importing literature, verify exact hypotheses and theorem-level applicability.

[Computation allowed/not allowed.] Use it for exact finite checks or falsification, not as a substitute for global optimality.

## Research directive

Do not return a survey of the theorem stack. Work on the exact endpoint. Before pursuing a lemma, write the implication from that lemma to the endpoint. Abandon or reclassify routes that fail this test.

## Required output

Begin with one of:
LEVEL I STATUS: SOLVED
LEVEL I STATUS: DISPROVED
LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN

If still open, give only:
1. strongest genuinely new theorem proved;
2. proof;
3. exact target advanced;
4. exact remaining lemma;
5. regressions and failed implications;
6. literature imported and its exact applicability;
7. canonical files that should change.
```

## 4. Audit-run template

Use this when another model supplies a theorem or proof.

```text
Audit the attached/pasted result against the current canonical state.

Do not reward novelty or confidence. Classify each substantive claim as:
- proved and promotable;
- correct but already canonical;
- correct only under added hypotheses;
- plausible but unproved;
- false/overstated;
- exact regression/no-go.

For every promoted claim:
1. restate it with canonical notation;
2. give a complete proof;
3. list dependencies;
4. check the relevant regressions;
5. state exactly what canonical endpoint changes.

For every rejected claim, give the shortest decisive reason or counterexample.

Do not rewrite the theorem ledger until the audit is complete.
```

## 5. Literature-run template

```text
The missing theorem has the following hypothesis signature:
[finite/infinite container, convexity, relocation, lattice status, quantitative parameter, desired conclusion].

Search specifically for theorems matching these hypotheses.

For each promising result, return:
- full citation and link;
- theorem number;
- exact hypotheses;
- exact conclusion;
- whether constants/rates are explicit;
- exact mismatch, if any, with our target;
- whether the proof technique suggests a new bridge.

Do not list loosely related papers merely because they use the same keywords.
```

## 6. Computational-regression template

```text
Test the following universal intermediate claim:
[claim].

First specify a finite model that is logically sufficient to disprove the claim if a counterexample is found. Prefer exact arithmetic. If floating point is unavoidable, produce a certified margin or a symbolic follow-up.

Output:
- model/assumptions;
- enumeration/search method;
- exact witness if found;
- what the witness disproves;
- what it does not say about global Erdős #99;
- script/test files that should be preserved.
```

## 7. Synthesis/promotion template

Use this when several long runs have accumulated.

```text
Treat the current canonical modules as the baseline and the supplied run outputs as candidate additions.

For each run, extract:
- theorem statement;
- dependencies;
- proof status;
- target implication;
- regressions;
- literature imports;
- failed stronger claims.

Deduplicate by theorem content, not wording. Keep the strongest audited statement. Do not combine incompatible configurations or embeddings implicitly.

Produce:
1. promotion list;
2. corrections/rejections;
3. exact revised frontier;
4. files to edit;
5. next continuation prompt changes.
```

## 8. Prompt anti-patterns

Avoid these.

### “Read everything ever produced”

This maximizes conflicting context. Read the active state and pull provenance on demand.

### “Solve Erdős #99; think hard”

This wastes a run reconstructing the frontier. Supply the exact endpoint and route map.

### Re-copying the entire theorem ledger into the prompt

It wastes context and makes the prompt stale as soon as the repository changes.

### Over-specifying a favorite proof mechanism

State preferred attacks, but permit a stronger route. This project has repeatedly advanced by disproving the initially favored bridge.

### Asking for “more progress” without a promotion criterion

Require a theorem, exact regression, sharper reduction, quantitative bound, or Level I result.

### Allowing literature imports without hypothesis checking

This produces false bridges between infinite-plane saturation, fixed-container packing, append-only problems, and relocation problems.

### Treating confidence as evidence

A model saying “this closes Target A” is not evidence. The exact endpoint and regression audit are.

## 9. When to cut a new prompt version

Create a new active prompt only after a meaningful frontier change, such as:

- a new exact endpoint;
- a major no-go theorem;
- a promoted theorem that deletes a whole branch;
- a new quantitative regime;
- a new required regression;
- changed literature permissions or source set.

Update `docs/prompts/README.md` so there is exactly one clearly identified active prompt.
